      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Architectural asphalt shingles provide the best overall budget value for most Utah homes, while standing seam metal roofing is the top-performing choice for heavy mountain snow, high winds, and energy efficiency. Utah weather demands materials that survive intense summer heat, freezing winters, and sudden spring storms\n\n\n.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "standing seam metal roofing"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2,
          3,
          4
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Top Roofing Materials Compared"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Architectural Asphalt Shingles",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Cost:\n$4.50 \u2013 $8.50 per square foot installed"
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Lifespan: 20 \u2013 30 years"
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Utah Pros: Affordable, handles standard temperature swings well, and Class 4 impact-resistant options resist hail\n."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Utah Cons: Can curl or crack over time under intense dry summer UV rays."
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              5,
              6,
              7,
              8
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Standing Seam Metal Roofing",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Cost:\n$14 \u2013 $22 per square foot installed"
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Lifespan: 40 \u2013 70 years"
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Utah Pros: Naturally sheds heavy mountain snow to prevent ice dams, reflects scorching summer sun to lower cooling costs, and resists high winds (140+ mph)\n. Ideal for higher-elevation areas like Heber City, Park City, and Midway."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Utah Cons: High initial upfront investment."
              }
            ],
            "reference_indexes": [
              9
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Concrete and Clay Tile",
            "list": [
              {
                "snippet": "Cost:\n$10 \u2013 $25 per square foot installed"
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Lifespan: 50 \u2013 100 years"
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Utah Pros: Exceptional strength against heavy snow loads and great thermal mass for insulation."
              },
              {
                "snippet": "Utah Cons: Very heavy (requires reinforced framing) and standard clay can crack during severe northern Utah freeze-thaw cycles."
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          10,
          11
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Making Your Decision"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "For Mountain or High-Elevation Areas: Choose metal roofing to manage heavy snow loads and prevent ice damming."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "For Standard Valley Suburbs:\nChoose architectural asphalt shingles (with impact resistance) for the best balance of cost and weather durability."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you share your city or elevation and whether you are dealing with a full replacement or a repair, I can help narrow down the ideal material or local considerations."
      }
